-> T26 Phase 1: `loop <var> { }` (no `over`) enumerates a scalar variable's
-> own finite domain in place — a is never resolved by this, before or after.
a ∈ { 1, 2, 3 }
loop a get doubled {
    yield a * 2
}
assert doubled = [2, 4, 6]

-> A bounded integer domain (from range narrowing on `Z`) is finite and
-> loopable the same way.
n ∈ Z
n > 0
n < 4
loop n get squares {
    yield n * n
}
assert squares = [1, 4, 9]

-> An already-resolved scalar loops trivially, once.
r = 5
loop r get once {
    yield r * 10
}
assert once = [50]

-> `loop x over <a Set value>` works exactly like looping over an Array —
-> a resolved Set is a concrete finite collection, not a possibility space.
s = { 7, 8, 9 }
loop item over s get tripled {
    yield item * 3
}
emit Length { value = tripled } to core get n
assert n.value = 3
